WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.590
Our school wants the agent to

2
00:00:02.590 --> 00:00:03.770
answer bus questions.

3
00:00:04.570 --> 00:00:06.970
But Darsi Tiny has never

4
00:00:06.970 --> 00:00:08.170
heard of a bus tool.

5
00:00:08.780 --> 00:00:12.340
Ask when the next bus leaves Kafi, and

6
00:00:12.340 --> 00:00:14.620
it looks Kafi up in the glossary!

7
00:00:15.190 --> 00:00:16.250
Can we teach it?

8
00:00:17.470 --> 00:00:19.850
Today: teaching an agent a new

9
00:00:19.850 --> 00:00:22.090
tool, and what it forgets.

10
00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:24.500
There are three ways to

11
00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:25.960
change what an agent does.

12
00:00:26.680 --> 00:00:28.360
Write a clearer instruction.

13
00:00:29.310 --> 00:00:31.870
Describe a new tool, which big

14
00:00:31.870 --> 00:00:33.110
models can often use.

15
00:00:33.810 --> 00:00:36.330
Or change the model itself, from

16
00:00:36.330 --> 00:00:38.430
examples: that’s fine-tuning.

17
00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:42.220
Fine-tuning means training a model a

18
00:00:42.220 --> 00:00:43.880
little more, on new examples.

19
00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:46.640
Its numbers shift until its

20
00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:48.300
answers match the examples.

21
00:00:49.110 --> 00:00:51.970
On your phone, only Darsi Tiny’s top

22
00:00:51.970 --> 00:00:53.750
layer learns, from sixteen

23
00:00:53.750 --> 00:00:55.950
conversations, in fifteen steps.

24
00:00:57.250 --> 00:00:59.270
In the lab, we teach it a bus

25
00:00:59.270 --> 00:01:01.670
tool, in English and Arabic.

26
00:01:02.370 --> 00:01:05.050
Before, it got none of eight

27
00:01:05.050 --> 00:01:06.130
bus questions right.

28
00:01:06.750 --> 00:01:09.630
After, seven of eight, even for

29
00:01:09.630 --> 00:01:10.870
villages it never saw.

30
00:01:12.030 --> 00:01:14.210
Then we tested its old skills:

31
00:01:14.890 --> 00:01:16.950
weather, routes, sums.

32
00:01:17.660 --> 00:01:19.920
Before teaching, eight of eight.

33
00:01:20.620 --> 00:01:22.300
After, with only new

34
00:01:22.300 --> 00:01:24.120
examples, one of eight.

35
00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:27.140
It learned the bus, and forgot

36
00:01:27.140 --> 00:01:28.680
nearly everything else.

37
00:01:30.040 --> 00:01:30.620
Quick check!

38
00:01:31.410 --> 00:01:33.890
It learned the bus tool, but

39
00:01:33.890 --> 00:01:34.950
forgot the weather.

40
00:01:35.650 --> 00:01:37.970
What would you mix into the training?

41
00:01:39.210 --> 00:01:40.450
Some old conversations.

42
00:01:41.590 --> 00:01:43.450
Practising old skills alongside

43
00:01:43.450 --> 00:01:45.090
the new one keeps them.

44
00:01:46.160 --> 00:01:48.640
With sixteen mixed in, old skills

45
00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:51.100
come back to five of eight, and

46
00:01:51.100 --> 00:01:52.780
with forty-eight, all eight.

47
00:01:53.410 --> 00:01:55.730
But the bus drops to five of eight:

48
00:01:56.350 --> 00:01:57.630
there’s always a trade-off.

49
00:01:58.870 --> 00:02:02.410
Fine-tuning takes time: on a laptop, from

50
00:02:02.410 --> 00:02:05.450
under a minute to over two; on an old

51
00:02:05.450 --> 00:02:07.950
phone, about three to eleven minutes.

52
00:02:08.670 --> 00:02:10.610
And it can change behaviour you

53
00:02:10.610 --> 00:02:12.670
didn’t aim at, even refusals.

54
00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:15.480
So test again, every time.

55
00:02:16.700 --> 00:02:19.340
So when should you fine-tune?

56
00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:22.960
Try a clearer instruction first, then

57
00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:24.880
a tool with a check in code.

58
00:02:25.540 --> 00:02:27.880
Fine-tune only with good examples,

59
00:02:28.540 --> 00:02:30.640
a test set for old and new skills,

60
00:02:31.240 --> 00:02:32.340
and someone to check it.

61
00:02:33.490 --> 00:02:35.830
In the app, ask about a bus first.

62
00:02:36.580 --> 00:02:38.640
Teach it with forty-eight old

63
00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:41.280
conversations mixed in, then test it.

64
00:02:41.750 --> 00:02:44.270
Then teach it again with none,

65
00:02:44.530 --> 00:02:46.050
and test the old skills.

66
00:02:47.390 --> 00:02:48.150
Your turn.

67
00:02:48.770 --> 00:02:50.910
A colleague fine-tunes the agent

68
00:02:50.910 --> 00:02:53.470
on her own examples, and the new

69
00:02:53.470 --> 00:02:54.590
answers look great.

70
00:02:55.310 --> 00:02:56.730
What should she do before

71
00:02:56.730 --> 00:02:57.770
the class uses it?

72
00:02:58.920 --> 00:03:00.980
Test old skills and safety too,

73
00:03:01.160 --> 00:03:02.740
before anyone relies on it.

74
00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:05.740
Remember: fine-tuning changes a

75
00:03:05.740 --> 00:03:07.500
model’s numbers from examples.

76
00:03:08.380 --> 00:03:09.680
It can forget what it knew,

77
00:03:10.040 --> 00:03:11.700
so mix old examples in.

78
00:03:12.320 --> 00:03:14.280
And test both, every time.

79
00:03:15.470 --> 00:03:17.890
Try it in class tomorrow: teach a

80
00:03:17.890 --> 00:03:19.930
new hand signal for quiet, then

81
00:03:19.930 --> 00:03:21.210
test the old signals too.

82
00:03:21.820 --> 00:03:23.560
Do it once with practice of the

83
00:03:23.560 --> 00:03:25.460
old ones, and once without.

84
00:03:26.140 --> 00:03:27.620
Which way did the class forget?
